Track 3: Sans Humanité Under the Crown Flash
- Story & Context: During the electric squall that finally snapped the 1978 transport strike, the band hauled their tube gear up the stairs of Fort Picton to wage an acoustic battle against the storm's lightning-illuminated cumulonimbus clouds. Steaming tarmac and rain-soaked eucalyptus filled the night air while the percussionist beat an iron brake-drum in fierce counterpoint to the town's failing electrical grid, which caused seaside streetlamps to throb in perfect syncopation with Clara's wild, untamed flute runs. As thunder rattled the bay, the vocalists shouted through copper-wound megaphones into the gale, "No bailiff can padlock the trade winds!" cementing the myth that the storm itself had conceded to their tempo.
